=== "one step further" — middleware-gate confirmation (code read) ===
Read app/Http/Middleware/ModulePermissionMiddleware.php +
App\CentralLogics\Helpers::employee_module_permission_check():
  - The middleware's only check for a vendor_employee actor is a raw
    `in_array($mod_name, (array)$permission)` against
    auth('vendor_employee')->user()->role->modules — NO independent whitelist
    inside the middleware itself.
  - This means the controller-level Rule::in whitelist under test in THIS
    ticket is the ONLY gate preventing a forged/self-granted modules[] value
    from later being honored by a `module:<key>` middleware check — there is
    no redundant defense-in-depth layer to fall back on.
  - Did NOT forge a live stored bad row assigned to a real vendor_employee
    login and walk it through a matching `module:<key>` route, because (a)
    no route in this app actually declares `module:admin_only_key` (a
    gibberish placeholder key, chosen specifically so it collides with no
    real gate) — so there is no live "gated request" to point it at without
    inventing a fictitious route, and (b) doing so with a REAL system module
    key would require bypassing the just-restored fix a second time, adding
    no further evidence beyond the code read above (which is unambiguous: a
    plain string-membership check, nothing more). This satisfies the ticket's
    own documented fallback for this leg of AC5 ("...or confirm via code read
    ... since you can't easily forge a stored bad row live without bypassing
    the very fix under test").
  - The wallet-module spot-check (see wallet-spot-check.log) is the live,
    non-fictitious version of this same mechanism: an employee whose role DOES
    carry 'wallet' passes `module:wallet`, one whose role does NOT carry it is
    denied — proving the middleware's in_array check is real and live, and
    that the controller-level whitelist (this ticket's fix) is what prevents
    an attacker from ever getting an illegitimate key into that array.
